                 DIGITAL'S AltaVista WEB SEARCH SITE
               RECEIVES AWARD FOR OUTSTANDING SERVICE


   ...technology to appear in a new family of software products...


SAN JOSE, Calif., April 30, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation's 
AltaVista, the fastest and most comprehensive search service on the 
World Wide Web, has been awarded Internet World magazine's 'Industry 
Award for Outstanding Service.' The award, a substantiation of 
AltaVista's remarkable performance from one of the leading Internet 
publications, will be presented at a ceremony during the Internet 
World trade show in San Jose, CA on Tuesday, April 30, 1996.  The
AltaVista World Wide Web search service was developed at the Western 
Research Lab in Palo Alto, California, where Digital conducts 
research on high performance computing and strategic technologies 
critical to the development of new information systems.

     "The AltaVista site on the World Wide Web is our technology 
showcase," said Ilene H. Lang, vice president of Digital's Internet 
Software Business Unit, "and we're very pleased that our work has 
been recognized with this significant award.  We intend to keep the 
service free to the Internet community, and will continue to make it 
even more productive for cyberworkers around the world."

     The Internet World magazine awards are presented each year at 
the Mecklermedia Spring Internet World Show and are selected by the 
Internet World editorial staff.  Louis Monier, senior consulting 
engineer at the Western Research Lab and one of the technical 
leaders of the AltaVista project, will accept the award at a 
ceremony to be held today at the Civic Auditorium in San Jose, CA.

     "With the World Wide Web's tremendous growth to millions of 
pages, finding information has become increasingly difficult," said 
Michael Neubarth, editor-in-chief.  "AltaVista brought the 
technology of searching the Web to a new level and was the most 
comprehensive among all the search engines we tested."

     Digital will shortly announce its plans to implement AltaVista 
technology in a series of fast search products for large businesses, 
small business and personal use.  The three members of the AltaVista 
Search product family -- Enterprise Edition, Workgroup Edition and 
Personal Edition -- will ship later this year.

     Digital's AltaVista search service (http://altavista.digital.com) 
offers both compact and detailed searches through the largest 
existing Web index currently available on the Internet.  At its 
launch on December 15, 1995,  AltaVista indexed more than 16 million 
full text pages.  By April 1996, the index had grown to include more 
than 11 billion words in 22 million Web pages and a full-text index 
of more than 13,000 newsgroups. AltaVista receives more than seven 
million HTTP requests daily.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scaleable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.  AltaVista software solutions 
from Digital provide high performance Internet connectivity to help 
cyberworkers quickly find useful information, easily communicate and 
collaborate over secure virtual networks, and transparently manage 
the remote computing environment.
