           U.S. ARMY SELECTS DIGITAL EQUIPMENT CORPORATION
                   FOR ARMY WORKSTATIONS CONTRACT


   64-bit Alpha Architecture Chosen for Third Multi-Million Dollar
                   Federal Contract in Two Months


GREENBELT, Maryland -- May 23, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced that it is one of two companies selected to supply 
the U.S. federal government with computer workstations as part of 
the Army Workstations contract.  Under the three-year contract, the 
Department of Defense and civilian agencies can purchase 64-bit 
Alpha workstations running the Microsoft Windows NT or Digital UNIX 
4.0 operating systems.  The delegation of procurement authority for
Department of Defense is $495 million.  The delegation of 
procurement authority for civilian agencies is $99 million.  

     "Army Workstations is the first of a new generation of 
product-focused, commercial-off-the-shelf hardware contracts," said 
James R. O'Neill, vice president and general manager, Digital 
Equipment Corporation, Federal Government Region.  "Digital's Alpha 
systems are establishing new standards for price/performance for 
today's open systems computing environments."

     In March and May of this year, Hughes Data Systems was awarded 
contracts by the U.S. Air Force featuring Digital 64-bit Alpha 
workstations.  These contracts were Air Force Workstations and 
Desktop V (five).  Air Force Workstations is a dual award valued at 
$956 million.  The Desktop V award to Hughes is valued at $924 
million.  The three, back-to-back awards (Air Force Workstations, 
Desktop V, and Army Workstations) within a two-month window, 
indicate the U.S. federal government's confidence in the power and 
performance supplied by Digital's Alpha-based 64-bit workstation 
product line.

     It is expected that Army Workstations will have broad appeal 
because the contract provides users the option to choose between the 
UNIX and Microsoft Windows NT operating systems.  The Alpha 
workstations on the Army Workstations contract will fulfill 
engineering, scientific, logistics, and administrative functions.

     Army Workstations is a firm, fixed-price, Indefinite 
Delivery/Indefinite Quantity (IDIQ) contract to supply computer 
systems to the Army commands, bases, and other worldwide users, 
including Navy, Marine, and Air Force users and civilian agencies.  
The contract calls for a 41-month ordering period, with a three-year 
warranty on all systems. 

     "The federal government has selected Alpha workstations, on 
Hughes Data Systems bids and now on a direct Digital bid, three 
times in a two-month window," said O'Neill.  "The endorsement of the 
Alpha 64-bit architecture by the U.S. federal government, the 
world's largest information systems user, demonstrates increasing 
market acceptance and demand for Alpha as the platform of choice for
today's mission-critical distributed processing environments."

